 >>[2026-11-06 17:24] observe — Probed seed at dbd138dd: a = 1 parses as three expressions (Identifier, an = Operation with no operand, Literal), and .{ a = 1 } holds the same three in its body, while a: 1 is a Binding. Lines opening key = value: 2,174 here, 25,429 in a mounted consumer, which writes them more often than key: value. a | b parses as one Expression with connector |. Filed p13 and p14 in the artifact; reading-drift gates both.
 >>[2026-11-06 17:24] observe — probe of wonder_syntax_hardware_alignment_spaced_equals, run after its ~#expect: the expect missed. Lines opening key = @handle or key = ~"path": 27 here and 2,590 in a mounted consumer (plan = @plan, roots = ~"./a.spw"), plainly aliases. Seed reads each as Identifier then a bias Operation whose anchor is the handle, because the = branch skips whitespace before taking an operator-led subject (expressions.ts, the bias-anchor branch), so 2,590 alias lines read as bias edges. Tight =@ and =~: 160 here, 0 in the consumer. The gap separates the two uses in both corpora without exception; p13's rule should also stop the bias branch from crossing a gap.
 >>[2026-11-06 18:10] note — p13 and p14 are held as owner trials before a verdict: interpretation-trials wonder_trial_spaced_equals (binding, identity, lean or loose; grounded in copular clauses) and wonder_trial_pipe (alternative, answer partition or pipe; grounded in question semantics).
 >>[2026-11-08 09:47] observe — Evidence for ?[register_from_container] from the site consumer (2026-11-08): 3,814 }[reg=facet] and 2,905 ][reg=set] among 6,890 stamps outside gen restate the shape of the frame they close, as here. A second mounted consumer's review independently proposes dropping registers for a pinned schema, and proposes one record form ({ key: value } with double-quoted strings) where the site writes key = `value`, the binding question of wonder_trial_spaced_equals. wonder_trial_clause_authority and wonder_trial_comparison are homed here.
 >>[2026-11-08 09:56] note — The owner, 2026-11-08: registers could be made more useful. Read with the 2026-11-08 stamp counts, ?[register_from_container] frees the stamp rather than drops it: the brackets carry shape, and a stamp says what they cannot. Six readings already compete (shape, bank slot, phase, normal-form construction, serialization sink, voice), held as wonder_trial_register; the journal entry 2026-11-08-extension-sockets names the register as one of four sockets for plugins, libraries and frameworks.
 >>[2026-11-09 17:07] observe — Ran seed parser and seed line reader over the same forms and three corpora (journal 2026-11-09-reader-agreement). They part on a body in two places. A dot: the parser reads host.valence.archetype as one name and .bread{flour} as a marked part; the line reader splits at every dot, so a lone .bread loses its mark (0 lines here, 1 in the site consumer, 38 in a second mounted consumer). A gap: the parser reads terms side by side as separate expressions; the line reader leaves all but the first unread (5 bodies here, 3 in the site consumer, 230 in the second). Filed p15 (an opening dot is a mark), p16 (a gap parts a body) and p17 (a shape is a line of holes) in the artifact. p15 follows the grammar and needs no trial; p16 waits on wonder_trial_gap; p17 lexes today.
 >>[2026-11-09 17:07] observe — The site consumer writes one line shape almost everywhere: 391 expression lines, 36 shapes, five covering 84%, every line word first in one slot order, and a dot joining 241 of 381 bodies. So the dot reading decides how most of that host reads, and slot-order freedom is so far a host gesture, never a typed one. Evidence added to wonder_trial_dot and wonder_trial_slot_order.
 >>[2026-11-09 18:12] observe — Evidence from both consumers, read 2026-11-09. For p16: the site consumer counted siblings side by side in bodies itself (850 and 243 in two corpora), and asks for juxtaposition and streams. For p17 and hole_as_name: a second consumer already writes a shared pattern as a spell with marked holes, and both consumers ask for holes. For p12: both ask for ranges with units. Also for p16: a second consumer writes space-separated values inside frames (a motif of six numbers, two tellers), the gap again.
 >>[2026-11-09 18:28] observe — From the consumer caches, 2026-11-09. A date written with hyphens is two errors to the parser and a whole concept to the line reader (wonder_trial_until), so p12 census should count dates. A second consumer proposes a mark followed directly by a number as a dial (~2 reads today as its own expression, and ~2 @8 as three), a body left open at the end of a line as legal and marked, and one node for observer, channel, ground and body, where ~{furrow}@keeper is two expressions today. Held as evidence; none is filed as a proposal.
 >>[2026-11-09 19:01] implement — Register stamps, at the owner request to make better use of them (2026-11-09), prepared in the working tree. Found first: a source stamp is read by nothing. The parser reads [reg=facet] as two parameters, the normal form keeps it as an attachment beside the register it derives from the bracket, and the runtime never sees it. Built: seed reads each stamp against its bracket and says whether it restates or informs; a census script; a hover that says which kind a stamp is; a function that removes restating stamps and no other byte. Census: 825 stamps here, 797 restating; 12,387 in the site consumer, 12,151 restating (about 124 KB), and the 236 that inform are shape words against another bracket; a second consumer writes none. No stamp was removed and no register given a meaning. Also filed p18 (a number is one token with a shape) and p19 (an arithmetic scope) from a table of 42 number and math forms.
 >>[2026-11-09 19:12] implement — register_stamp_reader landed as 988bd940 on feature/valence-registers, authorized by the owner; the same commit fixes a dropped first path in both analyzer scripts.
 >>[2026-11-13 12:56] observe — The number forms a tuning line wants, lexed and parsed at 61ac9bb6 with npx tsx against lex and parse in seed. The card and the 2026-11-09 journal already hold the sign, exponent, unit and plus rows; the ratio, proportion and frame rows are new. Read whole: 0.5 is one NUMBER; 3/4 is one expression of two numbers joined by the / connector, bare and inside x[ ] and %frame[ ]; 1..5 is the same with ..; 48<hours> is one capsule with 48 on its left; %gain[0.6] and %ms[45] are one % operation with a label and one number in its frame. Split with no error and no warning: 392hz, 45ms and 2st into a number and a name, two frame items inside x[ ]; 1e3 into 1 and e3; 0x1f into 0 and x1f; 1_000 into 1 and _000; .5 into the . mark and 5; 50% into 50 and the % mark. Read as something else: 4:5:6 is a binding of 4 to a binding of 5 to 6, and 12:30 a binding of 12 to 30; x[4/3 * 392] is three frame items, 4/3, the * mark and 392; x[1 2 3] is three frame items. With one error: 18dB and %gain[6dB], each written with a hyphen-minus before the digits, lose it and read as 18 dB and 6 dB; 2^(2/12) with a hyphen-minus before the first 2 inside the scope reads as 2, then ^ over a scope holding 2/12. To prose: +3 and x[+3]. A minus between name characters is kept: a-4 is one name.
 >>[2026-11-13 12:56] verify — What the runtime makes of the forms that parse, runSpw at 61ac9bb6. A number literal arrives as its spelling: the value of 0.5 is the string 0.5, since normal form keeps the token text of a literal (normalize.ts, reg literal; a quoted string keeps its quotes, so the two still differ). 3/4 evaluates to the string 4, because / is the projection connector and returns its last argument when the left is not a record. 1..5 evaluates to a list of 1 and 5. 1 + 2 comes back as an unevaluated + node and 2 * 3 as a list of 2, null and 3. None of the five was computed. Separately, runtimeMagnitude called on the string 0.5 returns 3, its length, and on the number 0.5 returns 0.5. In packages/spw-seed/src/math, grep finds no Number(, parseFloat or parseInt and no import of the lexer or parser: its functions take JavaScript numbers and none reads a number out of Spw text.
 >>[2026-11-13 12:56] surprise — Two readers in seed disagree about what a number is. The lexer takes digits with a decimal part. readEarcons (experimental/earcons.ts) takes the text inside the brackets and hands it to JavaScript Number, so %body[1e3] sets 1000, %body[0x1f4] sets 500, %gain[.5] sets 0.5, %ms[+80] sets 80, and %gain[1] with a hyphen-minus before the 1 is read as minus one and clamped to 0 with a report: five spellings the parser splits, errors on or drops to prose. In the other direction it refuses what relative tuning would write: %frame[4/3], %ms[45ms], %gain[6dB] with a hyphen-minus, %body[392hz], %gain[50%] and %ms[20..80] each report not_a_number and change nothing. %phrase[1/1 5/4 3/2] reports not_a_number three times and also empties the phrase, which writeEarcons writes back as =phrase[]. Run with npx tsx against readEarcons and writeEarcons.
 >>[2026-11-13 12:56] note — Census of written numbers, lexed over the 1,312 tracked .spw files outside _archive at 61ac9bb6 with a scratch script; a string is one token, so numbers inside strings are not counted. 6,848 NUMBER tokens, 937 with a decimal part. Tight against one of a short list of unit words: 41 (px 27, s 5, ms 5, d 2, hz 1, k 1; the list holds single letters, so some are not units). Followed by .. and a number: 31, most of them years (1712..1781). Tight n:m: 3 (3:4, 3:45, 0:10). Tight n/m: 1 (4/4). A number tight against %: 0. A minus before a number: 0 by the token detector, which fires on a seeded line; a text search still finds charge: -1 in one probe seed, and why the detector misses it was not checked. Other digit-led runs: 223, of which 188 hold six or more hex characters. Outside strings this corpus hardly writes units, ratios or signs yet; the demand measured today is the tuning line and the asks of two consumers on the card. The two mounted consumers were not counted.
 >>[2026-11-13 12:56] revise — p18_shaped_number_fixtures is split by shape into four fixtures, since each waits on a different answer, and five commits that are small enough to stand alone are named before them: a table that pins how the forms read today, the census as a script, a reader of one written value beside the parser, the earcon reader taking its numbers through it, and a number literal reaching the runtime as a number. Four questions open: ratio_spelling, unit_place, minus_sign and number_authority. Each offers readings and decides none. The card next stays p1_p7_verdicts; the number commits do not depend on it and may be cut in any order with it.
 >>[2026-11-13 16:40] hygiene — The two 12:56 entries on number forms quoted a minus before a digit bare, which is the lexer gap they describe: the stream parse-health test failed on them. No quoting helps, since an entry line is read as prose with figures and a hyphen-minus before a digit is an error there wherever it stands; the forms are now described in words (a hyphen-minus before the digits) and no claim changed. So a plan cannot yet write a negative number in a stream entry, one more line for ?[minus_sign].
 >>[2026-11-13 21:06] implement — number_form_reader landed as readNumberForm (seed experimental, spw.number-form/1): one written value read whole, beside the parser, as a number with its sign, exponent, percent and unit, a ratio of two decimals, or a range of two numbers; hex, a joiner in digits, a zero denominator, n:m and a doubled sign are refused by name. The lexer is unchanged, so no line reads differently. earcons_one_number_reader landed with it: settings lines and the ear line take every number through it, and a vocabulary word may list units with their scale (the ear line takes ms and s, hz and khz, st). So %ms[0.045s], %frame[4/3] and a negative value in brackets now read; a unit the word does not list is not_a_unit.
 >>[2026-11-13 21:06] decide — number_authority, as practice and not as the lexer's answer: every reader that takes a value from text uses readNumberForm, so what such a reader accepts is written in one place. Whether the lexer should take the same forms (a sign, an exponent, a unit as one token) stays open at minus_sign, unit_place and ratio_spelling, and the p18 fixtures still wait on them.
 >>[2026-11-13 21:06] verify — 22 tests on the number reader, one a law that it reads back 2,000 numbers as JavaScript writes them and 2,000 ratios; three mutants each fail a test. Seed 1,231 pass, tsc clean.
}

^["open"]{
 ?[evidential_glyph]: "Is #~ the right surface for the evidential aim, or does it collide visually with the ~# stance in dense stacks? Answer before evidential_aim_spike spikes the lexer."
 ?[latch_authority]: "Who may write $%[handle = value @rev] latches — only the cut gate --emit pass, or any S0 tool? Decides whether latch_diffing is a lint or a writer."
 ?[scoped_anchor_rename]: "Do #>.offset anchors survive a rename of the segment base without breaking existing ~\"file#anchor\" links? resolve-fragment.ts is the place to check."
 ?[grade_spelling]: "Should the sample spell probe rings !probe[S0] or !probe[effect.l0], given the code's EffectGrade is effect.l0.measure and seed-roundness-grades is retiring S0-S3 in living docs?"
 ?[register_from_container]: "Does a bracket say its own shape, so a stamp names only what the bracket cannot? Seed's reader counted 825 stamps here (797 restating) and 12,387 in the site consumer (12,151 restating, about 124 KB) on 2026-11-09, and the normal form already derives facet and set from the bracket. Deciding yes frees the stamp; what it then carries is wonder_trial_register."
 ?[spaced_equals]: ~"./wip.spw#wonder_syntax_hardware_alignment_spaced_equals"
 ?[pipe_meaning]: "Is | between terms the either join (p14), and does that answer a mounted consumer's | as a later mark without a new lexeme?"
 ?[gap_reach]: ~"../interpretation-trials/interpretation-trials.spw#wonder_trial_gap"
 ?[ratio_spelling]: "How is a ratio written so a reader can tell it from what the same marks already mean? 4/3 parses as two numbers joined by /, which the runtime reads as projection and evaluates to 4; 4:5:6 parses as a binding of 4 to a binding of 5 to 6. Readings offered: a tight / between two numbers is a ratio; a chain of numbers joined by : is a proportion; numbers set apart by a gap in a frame, as x[1 2 3] reads today as three items; a word that says ratio. Answer before ~[ratio_reading_fixture]."
 ?[unit_place]: "Where does a unit sit: on the number (45ms, which splits today), in a capsule (48<hours>, one node today), in the word (%ms[45], as the earcon line writes it), or declared once on a dial, as wonder_trial_number_forms offers? The ~#open of p18 asks about the first two; the tuning line adds the third. Answer before ~[p18_unit_fixture]."
 ?[minus_sign]: "Decibels and a step down need a value below zero, and - is not a token: one error and the sign is lost. Is the answer a signed number (p18), a word for direction, as a second consumer chose when it wrote %lead[4] for %delay[-4], or both? Answer before ~[p18_sign_fixture]."
 ?[number_authority]: "Which reader says what a number is? The lexer takes digits and a decimal part; readEarcons hands text to JavaScript Number and so accepts 1e3, 0x1f4, .5 and +80. Should every reader beside the parser accept exactly what the lexer accepts, or may a reader accept more and say so? Answer before ~[earcons_one_number_reader]."
 ?[hole_as_name]: "Should the parser read a hole wherever a name may stand, so a shape written with holes (p17) parses as the line it stands for? Today it reads ~_ as ~ and _ apart, and _._._ as five expressions where a.b.c is one name."
}

^["commits"]{
 .[init_plan_surfaces](5acc5545): ".[caches] — shelf the corpus and leave a resume note for the next mount (the plan surfaces rode in with the caches shelf commit)"
 ~[p1_p7_verdicts]: ".[plans] — one verdict per proposal (one wire, duplicate, superseded, or blocked), citing tree anchors (spw/theory/measures-and-handles.spw, spw/theory/epistemic-status.spw, tree/contract.spw)"
 ~[evidential_aim_spike]: "^seed[particles] — lexer spike: #~ evidential aim with #!route(...) payload, behind a failing-then-passing particle-grammar test; no parser law until the samples are reviewed"
 ~[probe_verb_lint]: "![cut-gate] — S0 probe verb lint in scripts/analyzers/spw-cut-gate.ts, scoped to the !probe[S0] form only (bare !probe{ } prose stays unlinted, see wonder 1)"
 ~[latch_diffing]: "![cut-gate] — $%[handle = value @rev] latch diffing: re-measure and flag drift between latched and live value"
 ~[derived_staleness_check]: "![cut-gate] — #~derived staleness check: a derived source newer than the file is a finding (needs evidential_aim_spike)"
 ~[dollar_percent_node]: "^seed[parser] — a dollar-percent metric port parses as one node, which unblocks p2 (spw_theory_measures_dollar_percent)"
 .[aim_names_in_plan]: "vocab[plans] — anchor, lens, and focus replace deixis, case, and mood in this plan's text and artifact (the samples never used them); the code rename belongs to directive-lattice; landed in the 2026-10-27 plan sweep commit itself, so the stream entry of 2026-10-27 10:20 stands in for its hash"
 ~[p1_tree_index_sample]: "^seed[caches] — a spw/index.spw sample where ^[tree] is the only child list (spw/index.spw lists children in tree, bundle, and ladder)"
 ~[alnum_word_spike]: "^seed[lexer] — p12, after the owner reviews it: a digit-led run lexes as one token with a shape kind (hash, hex, unit, name), with a corpus negative control over every other token"
 ~[p13_spaced_equals_fixture]: "^seed[parser] — p13, after ?[spaced_equals]: failing fixtures where a = 1 and .{ rank = 1 home = x } read as bindings and =@node{…} and =ref stay bias; reading-drift lists the lines that change first"
 ~[p14_pipe_table_entry]: ".[operators] — p14, after ?[pipe_meaning]: the connector table names | as alternative, with its hover"
 ~[p15_opening_dot_fixture]: "^seed[line] — p15: a failing fixture pair where .bread{flour} reads the same alone in a body and in company; lands as line-kit parts_as_parsed"
 ~[p16_gap_parts_fixture]: "^seed[line] — p16, after wonder_trial_gap: failing fixtures where *wonders{ @a $b #c } has three parts and a.b c.d has two; reading-drift lists the prose bodies that change first"
 .[p17_shape_sample]: ".[samples] — p17: samples/shapes.spw writes a mounted consumer's five commonest shapes as lines of holes, each beside one line that fills it; no parser change; landed in the 2026-11-09 plans commit itself, so the stream entry of 2026-11-09 17:07 stands in for its hash"
 ~[p17_hole_as_name]: "^seed[parser] — p17, after ?[hole_as_name]: failing fixtures where ~_ is one marked hole and _._ is one dotted name; reading-drift lists the selector patterns that change first"
